{"key":"enterprise/T1071.004","id":"T1071.004","name":"DNS","domain":"enterprise","level":"subtechnique","parent_id":"T1071","tactics":["command-and-control"],"platforms":["ESXi","Linux","macOS","Network Devices","Windows"],"environments":["cloud-hosted-endpoint","network-infrastructure","onprem","virtualization"],"environment_basis":"project_taxonomy_from_upstream_platforms","summary":"Adversaries may communicate using the Domain Name System (DNS) application layer protocol to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server. The DNS protocol serves an administrative function in computer networking and thus may be very common in environments. DNS traffic may…","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/004","source_stix_id":"attack-pattern--1996eef1-ced3-4d7f-bf94-33298cabbf72","source_modified":"2026-05-12T15:12:00.622Z","source_object_version":"1.4","telemetry":{"status":"mapped","components":[{"id":"DC0082","name":"Network Connection Creation","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0082","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--181a9f8c-c780-4f1f-91a8-edb770e904ba","page":"telemetry/DC0082/"},{"id":"DC0085","name":"Network Traffic Content","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0085","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--3772e279-27d6-477a-9fe3-c6beb363594c","page":"telemetry/DC0085/"},{"id":"DC0078","name":"Network Traffic Flow","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0078","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--a7f22107-02e5-4982-9067-6625d4a1765a","page":"telemetry/DC0078/"},{"id":"DC0032","name":"Process Creation","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0032","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--3d20385b-24ef-40e1-9f56-f39750379077","page":"telemetry/DC0032/"}],"strategies":[{"id":"DET0400","name":"Behavioral Detection of DNS Tunneling and Application Laye